#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# fourier_matrix

#% text
# `fourier_matrix(n)` gives the n x n matrix of the discrete Fourier transform, with entries n^((a - 1)/2) exp(2 pi i b (r - 1)(s - 1)/n) in row s and column r, so that `dot(fourier_matrix(n), u)` is `fourier(u)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The option `fourier_parameters: [a, b]` (default [0, 1]) takes the conventions of `fourier`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
fourier_matrix(2)
#% output
#= [[(1/2)*2^(1/2), (1/2)*2^(1/2)], [(1/2)*2^(1/2), (-1/2)*2^(1/2)]]

#% cell
fourier_matrix(4)
#% output
#= [[1/2, 1/2, 1/2, 1/2], [1/2, im/2, -1/2, -im/2], [1/2, -1/2, 1/2, -1/2], [1/2, -im/2, -1/2, im/2]]

#% text
# The matrix of the transform with the default parameters is unitary:

#% cell
norm(n(dot(fourier_matrix(5), conjugate(transpose(fourier_matrix(5))))) - identity_matrix(5)) < 1e-14
#% output
#= true

#% cell
chop(n(dot(fourier_matrix(4), [1, 2, 3, 4])) - fourier([1., 2, 3, 4]))
#% output
#= [0, 0, 0, 0]

#% cell
fourier_matrix(3, fourier_parameters: [1, -1])
#% output
#= [[1, 1, 1], [1, -1/2 - (im/2)*3^(1/2), -1/2 + (im/2)*3^(1/2)], [1, -1/2 + (im/2)*3^(1/2), -1/2 - (im/2)*3^(1/2)]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `fourier`, `inverse_fourier`
